196
00:15:10,633 --> 00:15:16,794
[SPEAKER_02]: When you say, I am so-and-so, you refer to certain generality of your experience.

197
00:15:17,194 --> 00:15:19,055
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you can say, I am this and that.

198
00:15:19,095 --> 00:15:20,735
[SPEAKER_02]: That's you rationalizing afterwards.

199
00:15:21,295 --> 00:15:23,576
[SPEAKER_02]: In the moment of designation, when you're speaking,

200
00:15:24,675 --> 00:15:31,057
[SPEAKER_02]: What you're referring to is that kind of a generally present experience as a whole, that's enduring there.

201
00:15:31,837 --> 00:15:46,462
[SPEAKER_02]: But yes, there are certain characteristics that are implied in you designating it with I am, unless your mind is enlightened, in which, as the Buddha said, even Tathāgata uses words like I am, this and this is mine, without those things really meaning that, implying the ownership.

202
00:15:46,962 --> 00:15:51,043
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're not the Tathāgata, means there is a degree of ownership implied in those things.

203
00:15:51,543 --> 00:15:54,367
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the first thing people do is, there is no I am then.

204
00:15:54,467 --> 00:15:55,108
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no I.

205
00:15:55,248 --> 00:15:55,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Deny it.

206
00:15:55,889 --> 00:15:56,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Deny everything.

207
00:15:56,670 --> 00:15:57,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, you deny the problem.

208
00:15:57,851 --> 00:15:58,812
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, there is no problem.

209
00:15:58,832 --> 00:15:59,013
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

210
00:15:59,577 --> 00:16:09,223
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that you can still speak in terms of I am this or I'm not this, means there is still the implication of that generality that endures for you that you are designating as yours.

211
00:16:09,904 --> 00:16:10,904
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's exactly the point.

212
00:16:11,365 --> 00:16:14,587
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't have the sense of I am without sense of mine.

213
00:16:15,227 --> 00:16:26,695
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't have the sense of I am, however vague, however careless, without the sense of ownership of that general experience that endures there for you, that you are subjected to, not subjected to you.

214
00:16:28,085 --> 00:16:32,549
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, when you're referring to I am, you're implying the ownership of your experience as a whole.

215
00:16:33,590 --> 00:16:45,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, if you say, okay, police the language, stop saying I am, because the Buddha said anatta, you're still regarding... you can... you can... Now, something else, a different designation, will take its place.

216
00:16:46,581 --> 00:16:46,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

217
00:16:46,862 --> 00:16:49,724
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's the underlying view that makes you assume the ownership.

218
00:16:49,784 --> 00:16:52,607
[SPEAKER_02]: Thus, your language sort of replicates it.

219
00:16:53,948 --> 00:16:55,209
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're saying I am,

220
00:16:56,469 --> 00:17:00,332
[SPEAKER_02]: You look at it as a symptom of, is there the underlying ownership of my attitude?

221
00:17:00,392 --> 00:17:04,354
[SPEAKER_02]: Not in terms of, oh, I must stop saying that because that's not Buddhist or something like that.

222
00:17:05,215 --> 00:17:08,417
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there is no sense of I am without the sense of ownership.

223
00:17:08,457 --> 00:17:13,560
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, if you want to really develop the experience of anatta, it's not about saying there is no self.

224
00:17:14,181 --> 00:17:19,284
[SPEAKER_02]: It's basically about abandoning the ownership of that which you are designating as your sense of self.

225
00:17:20,745 --> 00:17:23,867
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what would be then, what would the characteristics of the ownership be?

226
00:17:27,125 --> 00:17:28,266
[SPEAKER_02]: Characteristics in general.

227
00:17:28,286 --> 00:17:29,726
[SPEAKER_02]: Just ownership.

228
00:17:30,767 --> 00:17:31,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Of mine.

229
00:17:31,707 --> 00:17:32,467
[SPEAKER_02]: Belongs to me.

230
00:17:32,668 --> 00:17:33,348
[SPEAKER_02]: Belonging to me.

231
00:17:33,408 --> 00:17:35,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Could belong to me.

232
00:17:36,369 --> 00:17:36,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant.

233
00:17:37,890 --> 00:17:38,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Having this thing.

234
00:17:39,171 --> 00:17:40,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's another thing, isn't it?

235
00:17:41,832 --> 00:17:42,692
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't have.

236
00:17:43,913 --> 00:17:48,815
[SPEAKER_02]: You would not entertain the sense of owning something if there is no implicit pleasure.

237
00:17:49,775 --> 00:17:50,676
[SPEAKER_02]: Non-threat.

238
00:17:51,316 --> 00:17:51,956
[SPEAKER_02]: Familiarity.

239
00:17:51,997 --> 00:17:53,317
[SPEAKER_02]: All those things are rooted in pleasure.

240
00:17:53,337 --> 00:17:55,218
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't look at it, you know, like, you know,

241
00:17:55,835 --> 00:17:58,797
[SPEAKER_01]: You suddenly notice you've got someone leech on you.

242
00:17:58,857 --> 00:18:00,698
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like it's... you don't think, oh, that's mine.

243
00:18:01,358 --> 00:18:02,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Or cancer.

244
00:18:04,760 --> 00:18:08,502
[SPEAKER_02]: That which is yours, body, but that's not yours because it's killing you.

245
00:18:09,283 --> 00:18:10,223
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the alien on my body.

246
00:18:10,243 --> 00:18:14,085
[SPEAKER_02]: When you say, my cancer, you really deep down, you want to get rid of the minus of the cancer.

247
00:18:15,166 --> 00:18:16,727
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the... that's the... that's the key.

248
00:18:16,747 --> 00:18:22,190
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can you then take this reflection further?

249
00:18:23,972 --> 00:18:32,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Sense of ownership, sense of mind, is a necessary basis for me entertaining the sense of I am, as my own, belonging to me.

250
00:18:33,335 --> 00:18:43,599
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you were to then fully understand the sense of ownership, there is a good chance you'll be able to then not entertain the sense of I am on the basis of that.

251
00:18:43,639 --> 00:18:47,280
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what are the characteristics and further characteristics of the sense of ownership?

252
00:18:50,341 --> 00:18:53,442
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, as the Buddha said, regarding things as mine,

253
00:18:55,364 --> 00:18:56,124
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a meaningless mistake.

254
00:18:57,765 --> 00:18:59,206
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's nevertheless the mistake.

255
00:19:00,387 --> 00:19:02,187
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how do you rectify the mistake?

256
00:19:05,829 --> 00:19:07,130
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it that you regard as yours?

257
00:19:14,554 --> 00:19:22,258
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, when you regard something as mine, what are the characteristics of that thing?

258
00:19:31,617 --> 00:19:34,283
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, yeah, I mean, just sort of asking the same question.

259
00:19:34,483 --> 00:19:41,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Well... Can you say this is mine without this?

260
00:19:42,499 --> 00:19:43,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you say mine?

261
00:19:43,440 --> 00:19:44,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, okay.

262
00:19:44,120 --> 00:19:46,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's always in relation to a thing that's already given.

263
00:19:46,222 --> 00:19:47,783
[SPEAKER_02]: It has to be something that's yours.

264
00:19:48,844 --> 00:19:49,985
[SPEAKER_02]: So, which is first then?

265
00:19:50,565 --> 00:19:51,266
[SPEAKER_02]: The thing that's given.

266
00:19:51,286 --> 00:19:54,909
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it your sense of mind comes first and then finds things for its ownership?

267
00:19:55,069 --> 00:19:55,249
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

268
00:19:55,309 --> 00:20:03,555
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that sense of ownership is secondary to, is completely dependent upon, a thing which happens to be given in the first place.

269
00:20:04,196 --> 00:20:04,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

270
00:20:04,776 --> 00:20:06,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is on the level of how we started this?

271
00:20:11,119 --> 00:20:18,508
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, the particular sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, but the domain of those senses, it's pretty much already inaccessible to you.

272
00:20:18,548 --> 00:20:19,469
[SPEAKER_02]: It's given your subjectivity.

273
00:20:22,036 --> 00:20:29,583
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, your sense of ownership then cannot possibly step outside of that domain, of any of those domains.

274
00:20:30,004 --> 00:20:31,105
[SPEAKER_02]: It's fully within.

275
00:20:31,265 --> 00:20:34,067
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is then that truly can be called...

276
00:20:34,087 --> 00:20:41,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fully understanding that, you see that any sense of ownership I can possibly have towards any of this, is always going to be dependent upon...

277
00:20:41,975 --> 00:20:47,360
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only a relative ownership, on account of things they were given that you don't own, and relative ownership

278
00:20:49,103 --> 00:20:58,932
[SPEAKER_02]: Because relative ownership, it's not ownership.

279
00:20:59,012 --> 00:20:59,953
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not your ownership.

280
00:21:00,033 --> 00:21:05,278
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like recognizing a relative degree of control you have within the domain that you have no control over.

281
00:21:06,506 --> 00:21:11,227
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess just seeing that, actually, just how... Well, repetitively seeing what you've just done.

282
00:21:11,888 --> 00:21:14,188
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why the suttas would go into these repetitions.

283
00:21:14,268 --> 00:21:25,932
[SPEAKER_02]: Not like because they were repetitive for the sake of repetition itself, but because each repetition, it's a phenomenological description of how should your understanding unravel.

284
00:21:27,650 --> 00:21:31,372
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's not about figuring it out, like, oh, yeah, I got it.

285
00:21:31,892 --> 00:21:37,975
[SPEAKER_02]: It's about constantly reapplying the same figuring out on every possible or every relevant level.

286
00:21:38,495 --> 00:21:41,276
[SPEAKER_02]: In case of the senses, it's the six relevant levels.

287
00:21:41,796 --> 00:21:43,037
[SPEAKER_02]: So you have to keep doing it.

288
00:21:43,097 --> 00:21:43,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

289
00:21:43,357 --> 00:21:49,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you don't do it, it's enough to just ignore a certain level and assume that you know what you already know.

290
00:21:50,080 --> 00:21:54,342
[SPEAKER_02]: And to that extent, you are not free of that assumption.

291
00:21:57,283 --> 00:22:08,211
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, sure, you need to understand the principle to begin with, in order to know what to apply, as in, as we just described, the principle of non-ownership is more fundamental than principle of ownership.

292
00:22:08,271 --> 00:22:11,033
[SPEAKER_02]: Ownership is secondary to it, the right order of things.

293
00:22:13,215 --> 00:22:19,360
[SPEAKER_02]: But then it needs to be done repetitively, in regard to the body, in regard to senses, in regard to whichever way.

294
00:22:19,380 --> 00:22:19,920
[SPEAKER_02]: Look at the mind.

295
00:22:20,300 --> 00:22:21,121
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's do the mind now.

296
00:22:25,943 --> 00:22:33,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Isn't it re-adjusting your point of view?

297
00:22:36,448 --> 00:22:36,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Carry on.

298
00:22:37,289 --> 00:22:52,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing where that point of view actually is, in regard to things, rather than... because self assumes a point of view which is beyond things that are happening.

299
00:22:53,807 --> 00:22:54,548
[SPEAKER_00]: In its direction.

300
00:22:54,708 --> 00:22:55,268
[SPEAKER_02]: Right, right.

301
00:22:55,288 --> 00:22:55,588
[SPEAKER_02]: I see.

302
00:22:55,668 --> 00:22:56,409
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what you mean by it.

303
00:22:56,909 --> 00:22:58,130
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how would you do it?

304
00:22:58,250 --> 00:22:59,511
[SPEAKER_02]: And how would you describe that?

305
00:23:00,152 --> 00:23:09,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by seeing that those... that point of view is surrounded by things happening to it.

306
00:23:09,879 --> 00:23:10,119
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

307
00:23:10,499 --> 00:23:12,801
[SPEAKER_02]: That's... Sure.

308
00:23:13,041 --> 00:23:15,122
[SPEAKER_02]: But let's... let's do it like... let's do how we've done this.

309
00:23:15,963 --> 00:23:16,603
[SPEAKER_02]: Your point of view.

310
00:23:17,004 --> 00:23:19,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Just... and stop the sentence there.

311
00:23:19,145 --> 00:23:20,606
[SPEAKER_02]: So, point of view, point of view.

312
00:23:21,527 --> 00:23:22,588
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the point of view?

313
00:23:23,328 --> 00:23:26,851
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what suttas do, by the way, when the monks exclaim things twice.

314
00:23:26,951 --> 00:23:27,951
[SPEAKER_02]: First jhāna, first jhāna.

315
00:23:28,452 --> 00:23:29,312
[SPEAKER_02]: What is first jhāna?

316
00:23:29,552 --> 00:23:30,173
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, I stop...

317
00:23:30,233 --> 00:23:34,256
[SPEAKER_02]: I won't think about things that relate to first jhāna until I understand the first jhāna.

318
00:23:34,856 --> 00:23:36,958
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whatever you want to understand, that's how to start.

319
00:23:37,138 --> 00:23:38,299
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm designating something.

320
00:23:38,519 --> 00:23:42,302
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm throwing this thing in this direction, without being concerned with any other direction.

321
00:23:43,082 --> 00:23:44,663
[SPEAKER_02]: What it is that I'm aiming at?

322
00:23:44,783 --> 00:23:45,304
[SPEAKER_02]: It is that.

323
00:23:45,404 --> 00:23:52,349
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you say point of view, describe that... that phenomena you're designating by calling it point

324
00:23:53,484 --> 00:23:56,586
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics of that point of view?

325
00:23:56,966 --> 00:23:59,568
[SPEAKER_00]: You're the center of experience.

326
00:24:00,108 --> 00:24:01,189
[SPEAKER_02]: The center of experience.

327
00:24:01,269 --> 00:24:01,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

328
00:24:02,169 --> 00:24:06,312
[SPEAKER_02]: That's quite an accurate description in a way.

329
00:24:07,573 --> 00:24:13,637
[SPEAKER_02]: But, what are the characteristics of that center of that experience further?

330
00:24:15,778 --> 00:24:16,618
[SPEAKER_02]: Have you created it?

331
00:24:17,879 --> 00:24:19,020
[SPEAKER_02]: Or have you just found it there?

332
00:24:21,078 --> 00:24:26,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it is... it is... it is part of the structure already that's there.

333
00:24:26,979 --> 00:24:30,800
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can you, like, for example, if you have doubt, or have I created it, can you remove it?

334
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:34,941
[SPEAKER_02]: Can you exercise your will to say, no more of this center?

335
00:24:35,401 --> 00:24:36,321
[SPEAKER_02]: It's inconceivable.

336
00:24:37,242 --> 00:24:48,484
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, even the point of view shares the same characteristics as this I-domain, E-domain, domain of dhamma, domain of the arisen phenomena.

337
00:24:49,394 --> 00:24:56,782
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how you stop assuming the outside point of view, by seeing that point of view, point of view, oh, I have no control over this.

338
00:24:58,043 --> 00:25:02,869
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you think outside of the point of view, that's already within the horizon structure, as you called it.

339
00:25:04,691 --> 00:25:05,091
[SPEAKER_00]: It's...

340
00:25:07,906 --> 00:25:08,947
[SPEAKER_02]: The external point of view.

341
00:25:09,808 --> 00:25:14,333
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, the external point of view of ātavāda, it's not actually external in its content.

342
00:25:14,353 --> 00:25:15,174
[SPEAKER_02]: That's impossible.

343
00:25:15,715 --> 00:25:21,121
[SPEAKER_02]: It's only external to the extent you ignore that it cannot be external.

344
00:25:21,702 --> 00:25:23,564
[SPEAKER_02]: So, take ātavāda, ātavāda.

345
00:25:23,604 --> 00:25:24,465
[SPEAKER_02]: What is ātavāda?

346
00:25:24,925 --> 00:25:25,586
[SPEAKER_02]: Self-view.

347
00:25:26,530 --> 00:25:27,890
[SPEAKER_02]: Translate it whichever way you want.

348
00:25:28,271 --> 00:25:29,091
[SPEAKER_02]: Or don't translate it.

349
00:25:29,111 --> 00:25:29,691
[SPEAKER_02]: Use the Pali.

350
00:25:30,171 --> 00:25:30,932
[SPEAKER_02]: But then think about it.

351
00:25:31,092 --> 00:25:31,772
[SPEAKER_02]: What does it refer to?

352
00:25:31,992 --> 00:25:35,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it refers to the self that's independent of the five hundreds.

353
00:25:35,473 --> 00:25:39,815
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, you're describing and there is a phenomenon there enduring for you.

354
00:25:39,835 --> 00:25:41,435
[SPEAKER_02]: And apply the same principle for it.

355
00:25:42,176 --> 00:25:44,657
[SPEAKER_02]: The external point of view, the external point of view, external to what?

356
00:25:45,197 --> 00:25:53,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you realize the extern... external aspect of it, externalness, is only relatively external to the content of something else.

357
00:25:55,153 --> 00:25:55,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Independent, isn't it?

358
00:25:56,154 --> 00:25:56,514
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

359
00:25:56,775 --> 00:25:57,015
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

360
00:25:57,035 --> 00:25:59,137
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then you realize, oh, so it's not truly external.

361
00:25:59,817 --> 00:26:06,643
[SPEAKER_02]: And not thinking that, clearly, not clarifying that fully, you maintain the notion that it's truly external, that I'm truly independent.

362
00:26:06,704 --> 00:26:12,949
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't also that things are happening to me, within me, in a way.

363
00:26:13,570 --> 00:26:16,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Things are happening within my point of view.

364
00:26:17,654 --> 00:26:19,255
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's secondary.

365
00:26:20,853 --> 00:26:21,974
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not saying that's the truth.

366
00:26:22,674 --> 00:26:24,515
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that's the truth of... Sure, sure, sure.

367
00:26:24,955 --> 00:26:32,578
[SPEAKER_02]: But I'm saying that even in that case, in order to address that, you need to see that point of view first, correctly.

368
00:26:33,619 --> 00:26:39,262
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, there will be no division in a sense of in me, outside of me, in that fundamental sense of...

369
00:26:39,282 --> 00:26:39,862
[SPEAKER_02]: When there is.

370
00:26:40,662 --> 00:26:43,884
[SPEAKER_02]: When there is, your starting point is then, things are happening to me.

371
00:26:44,024 --> 00:26:44,404
[SPEAKER_02]: To what?

372
00:26:44,744 --> 00:26:46,105
[SPEAKER_02]: To this center of experience.

373
00:26:46,505 --> 00:26:48,526
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics of the center of experience?

374
00:26:49,608 --> 00:26:53,716
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, now, that's going into actually trying to understand it, that as a... Yeah.

375
00:26:53,736 --> 00:26:57,504
[SPEAKER_00]: ...someone who doesn't understand it, that's what it's like.

376
00:26:59,163 --> 00:26:59,783
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, okay.

377
00:27:00,283 --> 00:27:01,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's...

378
00:27:01,303 --> 00:27:01,984
[SPEAKER_02]: But what about that?

379
00:27:02,724 --> 00:27:03,644
[SPEAKER_02]: How's that relevant then?

380
00:27:04,384 --> 00:27:06,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, to know what it's like.

381
00:27:07,304 --> 00:27:11,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you're not trying to understand it... Yeah, but you are busy.

382
00:27:11,465 --> 00:27:12,885
[SPEAKER_00]: To understand it, you have to know what it's like.

383
00:27:12,925 --> 00:27:13,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

384
00:27:14,046 --> 00:27:15,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's not relevant.

385
00:27:15,786 --> 00:27:17,366
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's still the point.

386
00:27:17,386 --> 00:27:26,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you are trying to understand it, or you won't understand it, any of those divisions in me, outside of me, happening to me,

387
00:27:27,611 --> 00:27:40,603
[SPEAKER_02]: That's all fine, but if you want to understand it, you have to go beyond it, because all of those divisions that you talk about, are the result of you already appropriating the point of view, which you can't.

388
00:27:41,044 --> 00:27:44,987
[SPEAKER_02]: If you look at it closely, it becomes impossible to appropriate it, because it has a reason on its own.

389
00:27:45,228 --> 00:27:49,832
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're saying that only once you've understood it, can you understand what ignorance was?

390
00:27:50,112 --> 00:27:50,493
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

391
00:27:51,253 --> 00:27:51,433
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

392
00:27:52,154 --> 00:27:52,775
[SPEAKER_02]: In hindsight.

393
00:27:53,916 --> 00:27:57,579
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't understand what ignorance is while you're still ignorant of that ignorance.

394
00:27:58,820 --> 00:28:00,322
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's exactly the point I was making.

395
00:28:04,385 --> 00:28:12,232
[SPEAKER_00]: So, trying to understand it, you start revealing what's going on, which happens to be an ignorant thing.

396
00:28:12,332 --> 00:28:12,592
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

397
00:28:12,632 --> 00:28:21,100
[SPEAKER_02]: However, it's important, as we've done there before as well, to clarify that revealing what's going on can often become psychologizing for people.

398
00:28:23,617 --> 00:28:28,321
[SPEAKER_02]: But, in general, people think that if I just start talking about this, I'm revealing it.

399
00:28:28,541 --> 00:28:28,702
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

400
00:28:29,322 --> 00:28:33,426
[SPEAKER_02]: You must stay with the first phenomenon you started to designate and understand that.

401
00:28:34,046 --> 00:28:36,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, when you take that further, understand the next one.

402
00:28:36,989 --> 00:28:39,771
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I say, like, oh, so point of view is the center of my experience.

403
00:28:39,971 --> 00:28:40,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Wait.

404
00:28:40,852 --> 00:28:41,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Point of view, point of view.

405
00:28:41,753 --> 00:28:42,553
[SPEAKER_02]: What is the point of view?

406
00:28:42,774 --> 00:28:44,015
[SPEAKER_02]: Describe the characteristics.

407
00:28:44,395 --> 00:28:44,976
[SPEAKER_02]: You've done so?

408
00:28:45,216 --> 00:28:45,796
[SPEAKER_02]: Put it aside.

409
00:28:46,277 --> 00:28:47,318
[SPEAKER_02]: Center of experience.

410
00:28:48,599 --> 00:28:49,940
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics of that?

411
00:28:51,139 --> 00:28:53,962
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's how you get to undo your ignorance.

412
00:28:54,002 --> 00:28:59,768
[SPEAKER_02]: Not by figuring it out on account of ignorant premises, but by questioning every single premise that you started with.

413
00:29:02,370 --> 00:29:05,794
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, yeah, you would then understand how you were ignorant before.

414
00:29:09,063 --> 00:29:14,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I say, it feels like things are happening within me, and I've got to clarify each... That's fine.

415
00:29:14,706 --> 00:29:14,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

416
00:29:14,986 --> 00:29:16,387
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like, oh, you shouldn't say that.

417
00:29:16,407 --> 00:29:17,167
[SPEAKER_02]: That's absolutely right.

418
00:29:17,247 --> 00:29:17,628
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

419
00:29:18,028 --> 00:29:22,210
[SPEAKER_02]: Things that, like, describe your experience, oh, it feels like things are happening in me.

420
00:29:22,350 --> 00:29:22,630
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

421
00:29:23,231 --> 00:29:23,531
[SPEAKER_02]: In me.

422
00:29:24,111 --> 00:29:24,411
[SPEAKER_02]: In me.

423
00:29:24,852 --> 00:29:26,312
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics of that?

424
00:29:28,153 --> 00:29:29,034
[SPEAKER_02]: How does that feel?

425
00:29:29,894 --> 00:29:30,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Things happening in you.

426
00:29:37,683 --> 00:29:38,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Say, something's happening in you.

427
00:29:38,924 --> 00:29:39,764
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you...?

428
00:29:39,844 --> 00:29:42,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, actually, you surround the things.

429
00:29:45,046 --> 00:29:45,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Carry on.

430
00:29:45,766 --> 00:29:53,589
[SPEAKER_00]: In me, means that things are happening in me, that I am surrounded, I surround those things.

431
00:29:55,410 --> 00:29:56,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Like what?

432
00:29:57,151 --> 00:29:57,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Like what?

433
00:29:57,831 --> 00:29:58,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

434
00:30:02,353 --> 00:30:03,473
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, think of an example.

435
00:30:03,514 --> 00:30:04,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

436
00:30:05,154 --> 00:30:06,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, just my... everything is happening.

437
00:30:08,072 --> 00:30:10,534
[SPEAKER_02]: I think the most obvious example for people would be thoughts.

438
00:30:10,934 --> 00:30:11,915
[SPEAKER_02]: Thoughts are in my head.

439
00:30:12,035 --> 00:30:12,696
[SPEAKER_02]: Thoughts are in me.

440
00:30:13,757 --> 00:30:16,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm thinking more even with what I'm seeing.

441
00:30:18,440 --> 00:30:19,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, take any example you want.

442
00:30:19,742 --> 00:30:20,822
[SPEAKER_01]: It is my experience.

443
00:30:21,343 --> 00:30:22,584
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever is happening inside my head...

444
00:30:22,604 --> 00:30:24,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you're seeing now is happening inside you.

445
00:30:24,445 --> 00:30:24,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Inside.

446
00:30:27,268 --> 00:30:27,708
[SPEAKER_02]: Inside.

447
00:30:27,788 --> 00:30:28,569
[SPEAKER_02]: My experience.

448
00:30:29,049 --> 00:30:34,273
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that entails, that implies, then the opposite.

449
00:30:34,594 --> 00:30:34,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

450
00:30:35,454 --> 00:30:37,336
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can you experience that opposite?

451
00:30:38,932 --> 00:30:41,433
[SPEAKER_02]: If everything you can experience is only inside of you.

452
00:30:43,294 --> 00:30:49,558
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can then the opposite... can then the outside of you be experienced, if your own experience is always inside of you?

453
00:30:50,018 --> 00:30:54,941
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you then know what's outside, unless you experience it inside, in which case it's not outside anymore?

454
00:30:56,678 --> 00:31:03,462
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, when you think inside, you implicitly determine the outside.

455
00:31:03,842 --> 00:31:05,002
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not this inside.

456
00:31:05,223 --> 00:31:06,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Which you can't experience.

457
00:31:06,143 --> 00:31:07,344
[SPEAKER_02]: Which you can't then experience.

458
00:31:07,364 --> 00:31:11,546
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, can there be inside without any outside that's inexperienceable?

459
00:31:12,186 --> 00:31:13,027
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you realize it can't.

460
00:31:13,047 --> 00:31:15,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Then you realize also that this inside is all you have.

461
00:31:16,769 --> 00:31:20,691
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, any division between inside and outside are on the inside.

462
00:31:20,791 --> 00:31:22,432
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's...

463
00:31:22,712 --> 00:31:24,833
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just going back to the...

464
00:31:26,406 --> 00:31:39,873
[SPEAKER_00]: The basic experience is that self, then, is a thing which you don't look at, first of all, because it will fall apart.

465
00:31:44,235 --> 00:31:45,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It is not seen.

466
00:31:45,296 --> 00:31:49,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Self is basically not under... For there to be a self,

467
00:31:52,703 --> 00:31:55,364
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to be not understood.

468
00:31:57,004 --> 00:31:57,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

469
00:31:57,224 --> 00:31:57,745
[SPEAKER_00]: It's nature.

470
00:31:57,785 --> 00:32:00,625
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an ignorant, unseen thing.

471
00:32:01,766 --> 00:32:01,946
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

472
00:32:01,966 --> 00:32:03,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one assumes to be there.

473
00:32:05,667 --> 00:32:12,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Because as soon as you start looking in that direction... Yeah, but I'm still basically on the inside-outside.

474
00:32:12,069 --> 00:32:13,669
[SPEAKER_02]: How does that connect to that then?

475
00:32:14,089 --> 00:32:15,830
[SPEAKER_02]: The sense of self and the sense of inside?

476
00:32:19,731 --> 00:32:21,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because it implies that you are outside.

477
00:32:26,399 --> 00:32:28,821
[SPEAKER_02]: The inside implies that you're outside, or the sense of self?

478
00:32:28,841 --> 00:32:31,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that I am beyond that which is inside.

479
00:32:34,563 --> 00:32:36,104
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, okay, yeah, you can start with that.

480
00:32:36,764 --> 00:32:38,645
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let's use that as a basis.

481
00:32:39,506 --> 00:32:45,849
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is the characteristic, pretty much, of everything is happening to me, thus I am basically the outsides of this.

482
00:32:46,229 --> 00:32:47,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's implied instantly.

483
00:32:47,810 --> 00:32:48,970
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

484
00:32:49,010 --> 00:32:53,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Your kind of... Well, that's exactly the kind of the characteristics of the sense of self.

485
00:32:53,572 --> 00:32:54,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Indescribable.

486
00:32:54,133 --> 00:32:54,233
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

487
00:32:54,253 --> 00:32:54,553
[SPEAKER_02]: Indescribable.

488
00:32:55,115 --> 00:32:58,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Eternal, lasting forever, non-dependent of these things.

489
00:32:59,017 --> 00:33:00,517
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the whole point.

490
00:33:00,717 --> 00:33:03,358
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't negate that, but you need to understand it.

491
00:33:03,899 --> 00:33:08,961
[SPEAKER_02]: And you won't understand it by kind of not... you won't understand it by thinking on the basis of it.

492
00:33:09,481 --> 00:33:14,463
[SPEAKER_02]: You will understand it by undoing the basis of it, as in the undoing the lack of understanding of the basis of it.

493
00:33:15,790 --> 00:33:19,453
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the experience of things are happening to me is fine.

494
00:33:19,493 --> 00:33:20,414
[SPEAKER_02]: That's your starting point.

495
00:33:20,914 --> 00:33:24,957
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is that, as we said, sense of me that we described?

496
00:33:25,337 --> 00:33:26,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, there's a sense of ownership.

497
00:33:26,458 --> 00:33:30,642
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a sense of things are liable to these things happening.

498
00:33:30,662 --> 00:33:33,404
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, things are happening to this body, things are happening to the senses.

499
00:33:35,185 --> 00:33:38,748
[SPEAKER_02]: And you get involved by assuming the ownership of it.

500
00:33:40,073 --> 00:33:45,736
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you start looking closely, you realize you have no control over the domain of eyes, no control over the domain of ears, and so on.

501
00:33:46,856 --> 00:33:55,861
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, if you were to remove then the ownership, you would then remove that equal self-centered sense of I am and this is happening to me.

502
00:33:57,782 --> 00:34:08,587
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you try to sort of figure out internally and externally and then how you relate to it, it won't be on the same fundamental level of undoing the premise of self that you took for granted.

503
00:34:09,736 --> 00:34:13,199
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean, like, when you describe, describe the sense of self, as we've just done.

504
00:34:14,380 --> 00:34:26,649
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're already thinking, oh, sense of self needs to be figured out, sure, and it's happening to me, all of these divisions, all of these ways of thinking are already secondary to you have taken the ownership of it, and the sense of self.

505
00:34:28,858 --> 00:34:35,061
[SPEAKER_02]: Hence, every individual thing basically can then serve as a base for understanding the principle that would apply to whole.

506
00:34:35,921 --> 00:34:44,526
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're not looking to understand the principle, then no amount of individual things can bring the kind of the whole picture.

507
00:34:46,346 --> 00:34:47,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's already secondary to that.

508
00:34:51,009 --> 00:34:52,389
[SPEAKER_02]: So, things are happening to me.

509
00:34:53,050 --> 00:34:55,491
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not necessarily the best basis to start because

510
00:34:57,060 --> 00:34:59,201
[SPEAKER_02]: Things are happening to me or not, doesn't really matter.

511
00:34:59,261 --> 00:35:00,281
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a bit abstract.

512
00:35:01,121 --> 00:35:03,742
[SPEAKER_02]: Things are happening to me and I'm the sense of self in regard to...

513
00:35:03,762 --> 00:35:06,523
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, but then I would ask you, why is that a problem?

514
00:35:10,024 --> 00:35:13,645
[SPEAKER_02]: Presumably, whoever wants to understand this, wants to free themselves from the sense of self.

515
00:35:14,065 --> 00:35:14,265
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

516
00:35:17,273 --> 00:35:17,733
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what I mean.

517
00:35:17,753 --> 00:35:22,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you do need to know, like, what to ask for, and then it needs to be asked on the right level.

518
00:35:22,314 --> 00:35:26,075
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, that's why the instruction of another and utterance of another is necessary.

519
00:35:26,375 --> 00:35:28,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because people are asking, what is self?

520
00:35:29,116 --> 00:35:30,836
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't necessarily want to abandon the self.

521
00:35:30,876 --> 00:35:31,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

522
00:35:31,676 --> 00:35:42,459
[SPEAKER_00]: So... And so, by going in my direction, which I've said, is you always, ah, the self is this amazing, eternal, I am thing that's just indescribable and beyond everything, which it is.

523
00:35:42,499 --> 00:35:43,619
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

524
00:35:43,659 --> 00:35:45,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, in the suttas, often, when people

525
00:35:48,561 --> 00:35:49,602
[SPEAKER_02]: I am that I am.

526
00:35:49,922 --> 00:35:50,242
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

527
00:35:51,463 --> 00:35:53,544
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is a great... it is a larger phenomenon.

528
00:35:53,564 --> 00:35:54,265
[SPEAKER_00]: True, true.

529
00:35:54,805 --> 00:35:56,606
[SPEAKER_00]: But as if you do not... you want to...

530
00:35:56,626 --> 00:35:57,367
[SPEAKER_00]: The higher self.

531
00:35:58,087 --> 00:35:59,648
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the mystical experience is real.

532
00:35:59,808 --> 00:36:01,990
[SPEAKER_02]: It is higher, in a sense, than what your mundane is.

533
00:36:02,010 --> 00:36:05,232
[SPEAKER_01]: It's higher than anything else that you can possibly imagine, because it's... everything is within it.

534
00:36:05,272 --> 00:36:13,457
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you want to be honest, intellectually honest to yourself, can you know a higher self, unless you're experiencing it right here, right now?

535
00:36:18,738 --> 00:36:19,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

536
00:36:19,740 --> 00:36:21,503
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's just hiding its content.

537
00:36:21,704 --> 00:36:26,352
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, it has a less defined content, less mundane content.

538
00:36:26,853 --> 00:36:29,097
[SPEAKER_02]: But in its nature, it's still a reasoned thing.

539
00:36:30,395 --> 00:36:35,660
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why Sotāpanna's understanding is, whatever has the nature of manifesting, that's why it's impermanent.

540
00:36:36,140 --> 00:36:40,624
[SPEAKER_02]: Not, oh, not that he denies that some things manifest at a higher level, some things at a lower.

541
00:36:40,644 --> 00:36:41,284
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter.

542
00:36:41,845 --> 00:36:42,586
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what everybody said.

543
00:36:42,606 --> 00:36:56,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever the aggregate, like, whatever matter form, matter feeling, perception, so whatever aggregate is, far or near, low or high, personal, impersonal, present, past, whichever, he understands aggregate of matter is there.

544
00:36:57,118 --> 00:37:00,720
[SPEAKER_02]: He understands aggregative... as in, he understands the nature of phenomenon is present.

545
00:37:00,740 --> 00:37:02,361
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't matter what or on what level.

546
00:37:03,982 --> 00:37:14,629
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if somebody cherishes the sense of I am, unless they forsake the criteria of intellectual honesty, they're still not beyond hope.

547
00:37:16,670 --> 00:37:19,972
[SPEAKER_02]: In terms of, if they want to understand, they still have a basis for that understanding.

548
00:37:20,983 --> 00:37:27,750
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can be as mystical as you want, and proliferate your sense of self as far as you wish, and that's not really the problem.

549
00:37:27,890 --> 00:37:30,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Depends where you're coming from, what you motivate.

550
00:37:30,152 --> 00:37:30,552
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

551
00:37:30,893 --> 00:37:31,533
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the problem.

552
00:37:31,673 --> 00:37:43,205
[SPEAKER_02]: If you sort of never ever, and you choose that, never ever go in the direction of questioning it, or in questioning yourself in regard to it, that's the worst thing you can do.

553
00:37:44,291 --> 00:37:46,412
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can be ignorant and everything else.

554
00:37:46,552 --> 00:37:49,513
[SPEAKER_02]: You still have a thread of possibility to pull yourself out of it.

555
00:37:50,233 --> 00:37:58,617
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you are fully, willingly cemented yourself in your own product of your own wrong view, that's the worst thing you can do.

556
00:37:59,277 --> 00:38:02,738
[SPEAKER_02]: As in, by that I mean, no, no, no, I will not think about that.

557
00:38:02,899 --> 00:38:04,399
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would I question the sense of self?

558
00:38:04,439 --> 00:38:05,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sense of self is the greatest.

559
00:38:06,340 --> 00:38:07,480
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why you question it.

560
00:38:08,021 --> 00:38:09,581
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't need a reason to question things.

561
00:38:10,782 --> 00:38:13,503
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why the Buddha did say that the most blameworthy things

562
00:38:14,635 --> 00:38:16,877
[SPEAKER_02]: In the entire, basically, saṃsāra is the wrong view.

563
00:38:18,118 --> 00:38:22,401
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing else can come on the same level as bad as a wrong view.

564
00:38:23,502 --> 00:38:30,488
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's a direct reason why people do everything else that's unwholesome and that keeps them bound to the to the wanderings and so on.

565
00:38:33,250 --> 00:38:38,915
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if somebody is mystical, I am this, I am this great, I'm this lofty, I have this mystical experience.

566
00:38:38,935 --> 00:38:39,455
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, fine.

567
00:38:39,736 --> 00:38:41,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Describe the characteristics of that.

568
00:38:42,138 --> 00:38:44,418
[SPEAKER_02]: You had a mystical experience of this higher self.

569
00:38:46,279 --> 00:38:49,239
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you have a say in the manifestation of that?

570
00:38:49,900 --> 00:38:54,060
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you even create something, actually, when you think about it, from that, from nothingness?

571
00:38:55,061 --> 00:39:01,322
[SPEAKER_02]: How can you create something, before which that thing wasn't, and you haven't thought about it, because that would already be that thing.

572
00:39:02,022 --> 00:39:08,063
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're unaware, unknowing, unconscious of this thing, and suddenly you create it.

573
00:39:08,503 --> 00:39:09,884
[SPEAKER_02]: How can that possibly happen?

574
00:39:10,864 --> 00:39:11,564
[SPEAKER_02]: Inconceivable.

575
00:39:12,294 --> 00:39:15,875
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, the experience of the higher self that a person might have had?

576
00:39:16,315 --> 00:39:18,256
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I was subjected to it.

577
00:39:18,996 --> 00:39:24,878
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same sense, I'm subjected to sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts, ideas, experiences.

578
00:39:25,378 --> 00:39:27,999
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics of being subjected to these things?

579
00:39:28,419 --> 00:39:30,340
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I can't own them even if I want to.

580
00:39:30,900 --> 00:39:34,981
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, my lofty mystical experience of a higher self was just another arisen thing.

581
00:39:35,401 --> 00:39:36,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that, it's impermanent.

582
00:39:36,962 --> 00:39:37,442
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that,

583
00:39:40,350 --> 00:39:40,770
[SPEAKER_02]: Anicca.

584
00:39:41,631 --> 00:39:45,653
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this great higher sense of self that I had is this lofty...

585
00:39:45,693 --> 00:39:46,674
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, lasted for weeks.

586
00:39:46,734 --> 00:39:47,435
[SPEAKER_02]: I was in this bliss.

587
00:39:47,895 --> 00:39:48,595
[SPEAKER_02]: Cannot be owned.

588
00:39:50,717 --> 00:39:54,119
[SPEAKER_02]: Devalues everything that's based on passion, that's based on wrong view.

589
00:39:57,541 --> 00:40:03,585
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... in a way, it's a... it's... that mystic, then, is in a good position, in a way.

590
00:40:04,645 --> 00:40:05,366
[SPEAKER_00]: He's very close.

591
00:40:05,386 --> 00:40:06,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

592
00:40:06,547 --> 00:40:08,328
[SPEAKER_00]: But he just makes a lot of mistakes.

593
00:40:08,348 --> 00:40:08,788
[SPEAKER_02]: Close?

594
00:40:08,848 --> 00:40:09,769
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's the thing.

595
00:40:10,536 --> 00:40:15,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Close or really, really far, depending on the motivation behind his mystical view.

596
00:40:16,738 --> 00:40:24,261
[SPEAKER_02]: Mystical view in itself, yeah, it's a wrong view, but it doesn't necessarily mean the person who holds it is like, as I said, beyond repair.

597
00:40:26,342 --> 00:40:34,185
[SPEAKER_02]: He might be quite close, if he's not forsaking that self-questioning, even in regard to his greatest pleasure of his enlightened sense of higher self.

598
00:40:34,805 --> 00:40:36,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if he's abandoning all things,

599
00:40:38,444 --> 00:40:38,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

600
00:40:38,844 --> 00:40:44,967
[SPEAKER_00]: He's abandoning all things, all things, and then just sort of keeping hold of that great thing in his higher self.

601
00:40:44,987 --> 00:40:45,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

602
00:40:45,407 --> 00:40:58,492
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the last thing he... And that's why you can see if somebody who is refusing to abandon that too, is actually worse off than somebody who didn't abandon all these things, but hasn't developed that deeply rooted wrong view either.

603
00:40:59,853 --> 00:41:00,853
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's what I mean.

604
00:41:00,913 --> 00:41:03,434
[SPEAKER_02]: It's rooted in motivation, not necessarily in the content of the view.

605
00:41:03,454 --> 00:41:07,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you abandon that thing, you've abandoned all.

606
00:41:07,956 --> 00:41:08,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

607
00:41:10,040 --> 00:41:12,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, if you abandon that kind of...

608
00:41:12,402 --> 00:41:18,046
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one person abandons the thing that he's supposed to abandon, and the other person abandons everything else...

609
00:41:18,066 --> 00:41:18,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

610
00:41:19,087 --> 00:41:23,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Except the thing he... And what's that one person that abandons one thing, and the other person don't?

611
00:41:23,730 --> 00:41:26,032
[SPEAKER_02]: That's exactly the difference between sotāpanna and the suttas.

612
00:41:27,358 --> 00:41:28,179
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what I mean.

613
00:41:28,219 --> 00:41:32,202
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody has arrived at abandoning all these other things without abandoning the right... the ātavāda, practically speaking,

614
00:42:05,264 --> 00:42:08,586
[SPEAKER_02]: But if one abandons the right thing to begin with, that's actually bad.

615
00:42:08,606 --> 00:42:12,129
[SPEAKER_02]: Because in the long run, you will then arrive at a complete liberation.

616
00:42:12,489 --> 00:42:14,090
[SPEAKER_02]: In the long run, this person won't.

617
00:42:15,491 --> 00:42:18,353
[SPEAKER_02]: If Puthujan abandons sensuality in this life, that's great.

618
00:42:19,074 --> 00:42:25,078
[SPEAKER_02]: But if he abandons sensuality on account of deeply rooting the wrong view of a mystical experience, that's not great.

619
00:42:26,177 --> 00:42:31,978
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, his non-sensual behavior in this life can probably result in, you know, better rebirth next time.

620
00:42:32,418 --> 00:42:34,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Coming around, he's going to be in a better position.

621
00:42:34,739 --> 00:42:37,860
[SPEAKER_02]: But, fundamentally, the wrong view will be more and more obscured.

622
00:42:38,080 --> 00:42:40,560
[SPEAKER_02]: So, sooner or later, when that runs out, he's back where he started.

623
00:42:41,420 --> 00:42:52,983
[SPEAKER_02]: While somebody who has understood the Buddha's teaching, who hasn't abandoned sensuality, who hasn't abandoned sensuality yet, but has abandoned the wrong view, means he will have to

624
00:43:01,325 --> 00:43:04,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in a way, he has abandoned it.

625
00:43:04,890 --> 00:43:06,453
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that it's sort of fallen off.

626
00:43:06,493 --> 00:43:07,374
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he has uprooted it.

627
00:43:20,627 --> 00:43:28,989
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's important to recognize that when you read the suttas as well, often people get kind of caught up and bogged into content and, oh, this and this side, etc.

628
00:43:29,449 --> 00:43:33,730
[SPEAKER_02]: It needs to be understood on the level of the domains, on the level of the nature of things.

629
00:43:34,670 --> 00:43:39,212
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I have a mystical experience of a lofty self, like you couldn't put it in the words.

630
00:43:39,272 --> 00:43:41,152
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, that's your experience.

631
00:43:41,632 --> 00:43:42,892
[SPEAKER_02]: What are the characteristics of that?

632
00:43:43,012 --> 00:43:44,973
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop looking at the content that you couldn't put in the words.

633
00:43:45,379 --> 00:43:47,000
[SPEAKER_02]: Describe the nature of it for yourself.

634
00:43:47,740 --> 00:43:51,563
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there must be something there, otherwise, as I said, speech would not be possible.

635
00:43:51,723 --> 00:43:52,964
[SPEAKER_02]: You wouldn't be able to designate it.

636
00:43:57,627 --> 00:44:02,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, originally, we talked about the I, the arising of the I.

637
00:44:02,990 --> 00:44:06,012
[SPEAKER_01]: We talked about the characteristics of the I.

638
00:44:11,085 --> 00:44:22,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would you describe someone contemplating arising of the I, passing away of the I?

639
00:44:27,116 --> 00:44:27,376
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean...

640
00:44:27,476 --> 00:44:30,158
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, contemplating rightly, or somebody trying to figure it out.

641
00:44:30,178 --> 00:44:30,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Rightly, rightly.

642
00:44:30,779 --> 00:44:36,823
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because, like we said, so, you know, it's quite easy to recognize the arising of a feeling,

643
00:44:45,744 --> 00:44:59,674
[SPEAKER_02]: Whichever way you were to contemplate... well, whatever thing you were to contemplate rightly, as in arising or passing away of it, intensifying or fading away, needs to be done on the right level of generality, so to speak.

644
00:45:01,355 --> 00:45:08,520
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're not on the right level, you... again, you can get to it by trying to do it, but you need to understand that you can only contemplate

645
00:45:08,961 --> 00:45:16,263
[SPEAKER_02]: The arising of the I, the passing away of the I, not by the actual I, but on the level of the experience of the body as a whole, which is the saḷāyatana.

646
00:45:16,363 --> 00:45:16,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

647
00:45:17,543 --> 00:45:18,604
[SPEAKER_02]: And you pointed that out.

648
00:45:18,744 --> 00:45:19,564
[SPEAKER_02]: It's singular.

649
00:45:20,304 --> 00:45:21,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

650
00:45:21,324 --> 00:45:22,525
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not six sense bases.

651
00:45:22,785 --> 00:45:23,925
[SPEAKER_02]: It's six sense base.

652
00:45:24,305 --> 00:45:24,945
[SPEAKER_02]: It is, yeah.

653
00:45:24,966 --> 00:45:25,206
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

654
00:45:25,246 --> 00:45:26,446
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a base made out of six.

655
00:45:26,566 --> 00:45:26,866
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

656
00:45:27,346 --> 00:45:29,767
[SPEAKER_02]: But on the level of base, it's a one base.

657
00:45:30,047 --> 00:45:30,987
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a one phenomena.

658
00:45:31,107 --> 00:45:31,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

659
00:45:31,747 --> 00:45:37,129
[SPEAKER_02]: Within that, you can contemplate the direction of the I, the direction of the air, the direction of the... but not at the expense of it

660
00:45:38,034 --> 00:45:38,994
[SPEAKER_02]: But even that...

661
00:45:39,014 --> 00:45:42,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically, practically, the intensification... Intensification of body.

662
00:45:43,476 --> 00:45:45,777
[SPEAKER_02]: The intensification of the body.

663
00:45:46,437 --> 00:45:47,397
[SPEAKER_02]: How did you reveal that?

664
00:45:47,437 --> 00:45:48,838
[SPEAKER_02]: How did you become aware of it?

665
00:45:49,078 --> 00:45:52,559
[SPEAKER_02]: On account of something that's presently enduring on the domain of the visual.

666
00:45:53,359 --> 00:45:56,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Something so impactful, something so significant that you've just seen.

667
00:45:57,281 --> 00:45:57,661
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

668
00:45:58,281 --> 00:45:59,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even just like...

669
00:46:01,622 --> 00:46:02,883
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't really...

670
00:46:02,903 --> 00:46:03,683
[SPEAKER_01]: I had my eyes closed,

671
00:46:07,497 --> 00:46:11,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

672
00:46:11,739 --> 00:46:14,000
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

673
00:46:15,761 --> 00:46:16,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Something or other.

674
00:46:17,442 --> 00:46:21,204
[SPEAKER_01]: You then look at... or you suddenly... a bird appears.

675
00:46:21,244 --> 00:46:26,026
[SPEAKER_01]: You suddenly start looking at the bird and you're suddenly more engaged with the visual domain.

676
00:46:31,798 --> 00:46:41,877
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you get to discern whether it's arising or ceasing, if you get to discern it peripherally, without even having to attend what you're staring at.

677
00:46:42,789 --> 00:46:49,916
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if there is a knowledge of the visual domain, for example, persisting, that's already mindfulness of either arising or either ceasing.

678
00:46:50,256 --> 00:46:55,501
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, as we established before, neither arising or ceasing is something you are responsible for.

679
00:46:55,861 --> 00:46:56,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

680
00:46:56,182 --> 00:47:02,428
[SPEAKER_02]: But you are responsible in being basically aware, mindful of that which has arising or ceasing.

681
00:47:02,728 --> 00:47:02,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

682
00:47:04,845 --> 00:47:06,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the arising and the ceasing is...

683
00:47:06,986 --> 00:47:17,368
[SPEAKER_02]: So, peripherally, not losing the sight, for the lack of a better word, not losing the sight of the visual domain, peripherally, even without paying attention to what you're looking at, that's your gatekeeping of the visual domain.

684
00:47:17,388 --> 00:47:17,788
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

685
00:47:17,868 --> 00:47:29,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, so, those, basically, all of these domains, the eye, the ear, the body, whichever you can even conceive, the five aggregates, all of those things endure

686
00:47:30,851 --> 00:47:35,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And while they're enduring, they're either intensifying or de-intensifying.

687
00:47:35,372 --> 00:47:35,752
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

688
00:47:35,972 --> 00:47:38,072
[SPEAKER_01]: In a kind of relative sense.

689
00:47:39,573 --> 00:47:40,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Relative to each other?

690
00:47:40,653 --> 00:47:41,813
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's the wrong way of saying it.

691
00:47:41,853 --> 00:47:43,554
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's again assuming your timing.

692
00:47:43,714 --> 00:47:46,074
[SPEAKER_02]: Relative means you're comparing the two.

693
00:47:46,234 --> 00:47:48,655
[SPEAKER_02]: It's either like this, or it's either like this.

694
00:47:49,195 --> 00:47:50,235
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can't compare.

695
00:47:50,255 --> 00:47:52,455
[SPEAKER_02]: This is going the same speed.

696
00:47:52,475 --> 00:47:55,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, no, the feeling is stronger than the perception then.

697
00:48:00,205 --> 00:48:06,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize that those five things endure, enduring, they're sort of just there.

698
00:48:07,786 --> 00:48:15,488
[SPEAKER_01]: They will be either within themselves, from the perspective of that phenomenon, you will see either this way or that way.

699
00:48:15,548 --> 00:48:16,048
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing else.

700
00:48:19,549 --> 00:48:23,550
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, then, you realize there isn't anything, there doesn't have to be anything.

701
00:48:24,290 --> 00:48:25,670
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess that trying to compare

702
00:48:26,584 --> 00:48:30,185
[SPEAKER_01]: It always assumes a center that you can stand in.

703
00:48:30,205 --> 00:48:32,386
[SPEAKER_01]: That's independent of things that you are comparing.

704
00:48:32,766 --> 00:48:33,986
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, which is the self-view.

705
00:48:35,026 --> 00:48:40,388
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how, usually, people would misunderstand the Buddha, when he would list these things.

706
00:48:40,848 --> 00:48:44,669
[SPEAKER_02]: And he would say, the idea... Ah, so you, like, lay it all out in front of you and then you compare.

707
00:48:44,709 --> 00:48:47,029
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you analyze them and understand them.

708
00:48:47,190 --> 00:48:48,590
[SPEAKER_02]: And you're just maintaining self-view.

709
00:48:48,630 --> 00:48:48,830
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

710
00:48:49,568 --> 00:48:55,312
[SPEAKER_02]: That would be true if you could truly step outside of yourself, which you can't, which means these things need to be understood on a different level.

711
00:48:56,093 --> 00:48:59,935
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why if a logician reads the suttas, you would think, oh, this is just tautology.

712
00:49:00,516 --> 00:49:00,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

713
00:49:01,216 --> 00:49:01,797
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it is.

714
00:49:02,037 --> 00:49:04,158
[SPEAKER_02]: From a logical point of view, it's just mere tautology.

715
00:49:04,699 --> 00:49:07,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you all laid it out there in itself, evident truth.

716
00:49:07,541 --> 00:49:08,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

717
00:49:08,761 --> 00:49:10,943
[SPEAKER_01]: I think you don't have to be a logician to...

718
00:49:11,003 --> 00:49:11,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

719
00:49:11,824 --> 00:49:13,865
[SPEAKER_02]: But then the difference is, okay, so what did the Buddha say?

720
00:49:13,885 --> 00:49:16,467
[SPEAKER_02]: If you understand this, what's the result of that?

721
00:49:19,296 --> 00:49:21,798
[SPEAKER_02]: Annihilation of passion and anger, everything.

722
00:49:22,138 --> 00:49:23,239
[SPEAKER_02]: So, am I ever free from those things?

723
00:49:23,319 --> 00:49:23,499
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

724
00:49:23,919 --> 00:49:27,282
[SPEAKER_02]: Means, I'm not understanding it the way that we intended it to be.

725
00:49:28,503 --> 00:49:34,928
[SPEAKER_02]: But, if you don't think that far, then you would end up thinking you understood the suttas, while still not being free from suffering and everything else.

726
00:49:35,108 --> 00:49:38,972
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I have developed an understanding of these things, but that's not what's being spoken about.

727
00:49:39,032 --> 00:49:39,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

728
00:49:39,572 --> 00:49:45,357
[SPEAKER_02]: There must be more, because I'm not getting the results that this understanding seems to say you should be having.

729
00:49:51,604 --> 00:49:52,133
It's not like that.

730
00:49:52,153 --> 00:49:52,560
It's not like that.

